Strict alignment: prove accesses through fields of promoted structs aligned (applies after perf-67).

perf-67 finds an alignment for every local whose values all come from GT_STORE_LCL_VAR, but it skips
promoted structs and their fields. A span local made from an array is promoted, so its reference is a
field local, and every access through that reference gets the andi/bnez test, also when the span
starts at an aligned offset of the array.

A field of an independently promoted struct is now a candidate as well, when the struct is not a
parameter and its address is not exposed. A store to the struct as a whole (a call that returns it,
a partial store) or a GT_LCL_ADDR of the struct defines its fields out of sight, so it removes all of
its fields from the candidates. Then every value of a candidate field comes from a store to the field
itself, and the fixed point of perf-67 applies to it without change.

Nethermind stateless guest on SP1 (sp1-runner v6.9.0), outputs identical:
  block 26279462: 82,765,142 -> 82,471,435 cycles (-0.35%), gas -0.14%
  block 26279465: 527,074,561 -> 526,681,175 cycles (-0.07%), gas -0.06%
  block 26279480: 319,858,795 -> 319,282,123 cycles (-0.18%), gas -0.10%
On 26279462 the executed alignment tests go from 1.19M to 1.05M.
Only builds with JitRiscV64StrictAlign change.

